<BaseLayout title="Migration QA checker | Kyle Murray" description="A local Python prototype that checks URL migrations and initial-HTML metadata, with CSV reports and repeatable synthetic validation.">
<main class="web-ops-page">
<section class="hero">
<p class="eyebrow">Python · Working prototype · Synthetic validation</p>
<h1>Make migration exceptions easier to review.</h1>
<p class="lede">A CSV-driven checker turns old-to-new URL mappings into a report of redirect, destination, and metadata findings.</p>
<p><a href="/downloads/migration-checker.zip" download>Download Migration Checker (ZIP)</a> · <a href="https://github.com/thekylemurray/ai-search-lab/tree/main/tools/migration-checker">Inspect the Python source</a></p>
</section>
<section class="ops-section"><h2>The problem</h2><p>Migration validation involves repeated checks: does an old URL reach its intended replacement, does that page load, and does its metadata match the plan? This prototype makes those checks repeatable and keeps exceptions visible for a human reviewer.</p></section>
<section class="ops-section"><h2>How it works</h2><ol><li>Open the local browser interface and load a CSV of old/new URL pairs.</li><li>Optionally provide expected title, H1, and canonical values.</li><li>Run the checks and review PASS, FAIL, and REVIEW findings.</li><li>Download a CSV with observed statuses, redirect paths, metadata, and UTC check times.</li></ol><p>The tool uses Python's standard library and makes HTTP requests from your computer. It reads initial HTML; it does not execute page JavaScript. Python 3.10 or later is required. On Windows, extract the ZIP and double-click start-windows.cmd when the Python launcher is installed.</p></section>
<section class="ops-section"><h2>Repeatable demonstration</h2><p>Choose “Load synthetic demo” to run eight local scenarios. The expected summary is one PASS, three FAIL, and four REVIEW results.</p><ul><li>PASS: a permanent redirect to the expected page and metadata.</li><li>FAIL: a wrong redirect destination, a missing destination, and incorrect title/H1/canonical values.</li><li>REVIEW: a temporary redirect, a multi-hop chain, a blocked response, and a redirect loop.</li></ul><p>Automated tests exercise these real HTTP responses, CSV validation, metadata extraction, report generation, and the local interface. The examples are deliberately constructed test fixtures, not client migration results.</p></section>
<section class="ops-section"><h2>A twenty-page migration exercise</h2><p>The download also includes a fictional community website with original, flawed and corrected versions. Launch start-exercise-windows.cmd to compare pages, download URL mappings and practice reviewing the findings.</p><p>Ten defects are deliberately seeded: the checker flags eight, while missing body content and a missing meta description remain manual-review tasks. Ten additional pages act as clean controls. Automated local verification produced 12 PASS, 5 FAIL and 3 REVIEW results for the flawed version, then 20 PASS for the corrected version.</p><p>This is a constructed learning exercise, not a production accuracy estimate. The corrected version is supplied; personal hands-on review and remediation evidence have not yet been recorded. Instructions and an answer key are included in EXERCISE.md.</p></section>
<section class="ops-section"><h2>Scope and next validation</h2><p>This first version demonstrates Python automation and QA decision-making. It was built with AI assistance as a practical learning project. A blocked response is an investigation item, not automatic proof of a broken site.</p><p>An owner-supplied browser screenshot and exported CSV confirmed the eight expected demo outcomes on October 16, 2026. A narrow Result column observed in the screenshot was corrected afterward; that styling change still needs a browser recheck. The specific launcher and Python version were not captured. The tool does not assess full content parity, visual layout, indexing, JavaScript-rendered content, or an entire migration. No time savings have been measured. Start with the synthetic demo before using a small mapping from a site you manage.</p></section>
